{
  "dom_id": "step-q2434",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Suppose that $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-t$ represents the position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line at $t$ seconds. What is the average rate of change of $s(t)$ from $t=1$ to $t=3?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-t$ be the position of the race car in feet after t seconds. To find the average rate of change of $s(t)$ from $t=1$ to $t=3,$ we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[1,3]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δs}{Δt} & =\\frac{s(b)-s(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(3)-s(1)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{[2(3)^{3}-(3)]-[2(1)^{3}-(1)]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{51-1}{2} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of change of $s(t)$ is $25$ feet per second.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
